articles.description:
Next.js App Router introduced two advanced routing primitives:
Parallel Routes
Intercepting Routes
These aren’t random features — they were invented to solve challenges that naturally arise in file-based routing.
This guide explains Parallel Routes from scratch (no use case at first), then naturally introduces the real-world modal use case, shows why Parallel Routes fail alone, and how Intercepting Routes solve that problem. Finally, we’ll cover all interception patterns (.), (..), (..)(..), (...) with real URLs — and end with practical use cases.
Let’s begin.
Parallel Routes allow a layout to render multiple independent route segments at the same time.
Example layout:
export default function RootLayout({ children, modal }) {
  return (
    <>
      {children}
      {modal}
    </>
  );
}
